2 CONT '''Nezahualcoyotl''' (Classical Nahuatl: Nezahualcoyōtl, pronounced [nesawaɬˈkojoːtɬ], meaning "Coyote in fast" or "Coyote who Fasts")[1](April 28, 1402 – June 4, 1472) was a philosopher, warrior, architect, poet and ruler (tlatoa
2 CONC ni) of the city-state of Texcoco in pre-Columbian Mexico. Unlike other high-profile Mexican figures from the century preceding the Spanish Conquest, Nezahualcoyotl was not an Aztec; his people were the Acolhua, another Nahuan peop
2 CONC le settled in the eastern part of the Valley of Mexico, settling on the eastern side of Lake Texcoco.
2 CONT
2 CONT More...http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nezahualcoyotl
