1 NAME Huanitzin 15a Tlatoani de Tenochtitlan
2 GIVN Huanitzin
2 NSFX 15a Tlatoani de Tenochtitlan
2 NICK Diego de Alvarado Huanitzin
1 NAME Don Diego de Alvarado, 15th Aztec Emperor
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1541
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Huanitzin''' (or Panitzin) aka Don Diego de Alvarado was a 16th century Nahua noble. A grandson of Axayacatl, Nephew of tlatoani Moctezuma II. He was initially the tlatoani (ruler) of Ecatepec before becoming tl
2 CONC atoani of Tenochtitlan, as well as its first governor under the colonial Spanish system of government.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had been designated governor (tlatoani) of Ecatépec by Moctezuma, in the year 2 Técpatl after the death of Chimalpilli the former tlatoani. Moctezuma was already prisoner of Cortés in Tenochtitlan, the people of Ecatepec accept
2 CONC ed him as their ruler and hid him along with his mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the fall of Tenochtitlan, he was one of the five Aztec lords held captive by Cortés along with Cuauhtemoc, the cihuacohuatl Tlacotzin, Oquiztzin, and Motelchiuhtzin. Along them he was also tortured, with his feet burned, bec
2 CONC ause of the gold lost by the Spaniards when they had to flee Tenochtitlan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Huanitzin was baptized with the Spanish Christian name Diego, and took the surname de Alvarado from his baptismal sponsor — probably Pedro de Alvarado or one of his brothers[1], whose uncle with whom they came to America was named
2 CONC  Diego de Alvarado.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cortés took Huanitzin along with many other indigenous rulers in his travel to Honduras. He was spared from execution when Cuauhtemoc was hanged by Cortés along with Tetlepanquetzatzin, tlatoani of Tlacopan and don Pedro Cohuanaco
2 CONC chtzin. After the return of Cortés, Huanitzin was released and returned as Tlatoani of Ecatepec, where he ruled 14 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT As grandson of a former Tlatoani, in the year 7 Tochtli (1538), he was chosen as the first governor of Tenochtitlan (Mexico), by the don Antonio de Mendoza, first viceroy of México. Tenochtitlan had been without official ruler for
2 CONC  almost a year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Diego de Alvarado Huanitzin died in 1541. Among his children were 
2 CONT *1. doña Juan de Alvarado, who married Huehue Totoquihuaztli, ruler of Tlacopan; 
2 CONT *2. don Cristóbal de Guzmán Cecetzin, who later became governor of Tenochtitlan; 
2 CONT *3. don Hernando de Alvarado Tezozomoc, an interpreter known today for the Crónica mexicayotl; and 
2 CONT *4. doña Isabel, who married Antonio Valeriano, who would also become governor of Tenochtitlan.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT == Mass of St. Gregory ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Huanitzin may have created a featherwork representation of the Mass of Saint Gregory, after a Dutch engraving. Dated 1539, it is the earliest dated work of art in New Spain.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT == Notes ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # Lockhart (1992), p.123.
2 CONT # Chimalpahin (1997), vol. 1, pp.172–173.
2 CONT # Holland, Tracey (2000). "Painting and Mosaic as Didactic Material". Didactic Materials in New Spain at the Time of the Conquest.
2 CONT
