1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Archivo General de Indias''' (Sevilla, España)  -  Coleccionista; Consejo de Indias (España); Código de Referencia: ES.41091.AGI/29.5.11.29//PATRONATO,80,N.5,R.1; Titulo Nombre atribuido:  '''Información de los 
2 CONC méritos y servicios de Juanes de Tolosa, Cristóbal Zaldívar,etc'''; '''1593'''; 301 pages (on line as follows)  ...  http://pares.mcu.es/ParesBusquedas/servlets/Control_servlet?accion=4&appOrigen=ext&txt_id_desc_ud=123141
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Founded the City of Zacatecas with three other men--Diego de Ibarra, Cristóbal Oñate, and Baltazar Temiño de Bañuelos.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan de Tolosa y su esposa Leonor Cortés Moctezuma se dice habitaron Nochistlán, Zacatecas, población colindante con Teocaltiche, Jalisco, y muy cerca del estado de Aguascalientes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mentioned in '''''The Last Conquistador''''' by Marc Simmons (U. of Oklahoma Press, Norman, Oklahoma, 1991).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''_________________________________'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Wikipedia'''''  ...  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_de_Tolosa
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Juan de Tolosa''' (fl. 16th century) was a Spanish Basque conquistador. He discovered rich silver deposits near the present day city of Zacatecas, Mexico, in 1546.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan de Tolosa was born in the sixteenth century in or near Tolosa, Spain. He took the nickname of "Barbalonga" in recognition of his impressive beard.[3] He emigrated to New Spain (later known as Mexico).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Founder of Zacatecas'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Tolosa' date of arrival in the Americas is unknown although he was apparently a soldier during the Mixtón War from 1540 to 1542. After the war, Tolosa led several expeditions in search of silver along with Miguel de Ibarra and a c
2 CONC ontingent of Spanish soldiers and Indian slaves. In what is now Tlaltenango, Indians showed him rocks containing silver and, on September 8, 1546, he found his way to the origin of the rocks on the Cerro de la Bufa at Zacatecas. W
2 CONC ith Ibarra he collected a group of miners and returned to the site to exploit the silver deposits which turned out to be the richest in Mexico. On January 20, 1548 Tolosa along with Diego de Ibarra, Cristóbal de Oñate, and Baltaza
2 CONC r de Bañuelos Temiño officially founded what is now the city of Zacatecas, later known as the "Civilized North."[4] This city is recognized today by UNESCO as a World Heritage Site.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Tolosa also claimed credit for discovering rich deposits of silver at San Martin, Sombrerete, and Avino 115 miles north-west of Zacatecas in 1556. Along with Luis Cortés, the son of conquistador Hernán Cortés, and 40 soldiers he r
2 CONC eportedly explored for six months at his own cost before finding the silver deposit. His primacy in the discovery of the silver at San Martin has been disputed. On another expedition he made an important discovery of salt deposits
2 CONC  at Salinas de Santa Maria.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Tolosa was apparently not as good a businessman as he was explorer, as he owned fewer mines and smelters than other early mining entrepreneurs in Zacatecas. In 1594, his children and other witnesses claimed that he had died in pov
2 CONC erty, spending the fortune he had made in Zacatecas on expeditions to find new mines. His children, left "without means and in need" requested assistance from the Spanish crown in recognition of his contributions.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Personal life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1550, he married Leonor Cortés Moctezuma, born out of wedlock and the daughter of Isabel Moctezuma and Hernán Cortés. She had a son, Juan de Tolosa Cortés Moctezuma, and two daughters, Isabel de Tolosa Cortés de Moctezuma who m
2 CONC arried Juan de Oñate Salazar and Leonor de Tolosa Cortés Moctezuma who married Cristobal de Zaldivar Mendoza.[8] Other daughters, names unknown, were said to be in a monastery in Seville, Spain in 1604.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Death'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The date of Tolosa's death is unknown although both he and his wife died before 1594.[10] In 1576, a Matías de Tolosa, requested an emigration licence in order to assist his "rich brother Juan" in New Spain. The request is accompa
2 CONC nied by a letter written from Acapulco which informs us of a good personal network by Juan which makes an identification likely.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Canudas, Enrique (2005). Las venas de plata en la historia de México: síntesis de historia económica, Siglo XIX, Volume 1. Universidad Juárez Autónoma de Tabasco. p. 186. ISBN 970-94243-1-9.
2 CONT
2 CONT Himmerich y Valencia, Robert (1996). The Encomenderos of New Spain, 1521-1555. University of Texas Press. p. 147. ISBN 0-292-73108-6.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chipman p. xxii
2 CONT
2 CONT Bakewell, P. J. Silver Mining and Society in Colonial Mexico: Zacatecas, 1546-1700. Cambridge: Cambridge U Press, 1971, pp. 4-14
2 CONT
2 CONT http://whc.unesco.org/en/list/676, accessed Dec 27, 2010
2 CONT
2 CONT Bakewell, pp. 23-24; Chipman, Donald E. Moctezuma's Children: Aztec Royalty under Spanish Rule Austin: U of TX Press, 2005, pp. pp. 102-104
2 CONT
2 CONT Chipman, p. 104
2 CONT
2 CONT Chipman, p. 106
2 CONT
