1 OCCU Soldier, Colonization of New Mexico  Member of 1598 Onate 1598 (Age 40 years), Military  was a soldier with Oñate in 1598. He was small of stature, gray-bearded, had a wart on left side of face yes, Member Oñate 1598 Entrada, Sold
2 CONC ier with Onate in 1598
1 NOTE {geni:about_me}     * BIRTH: 1558, Ciudád de Mexico, Nueva España [3226]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * DEATH: AFT 1626 [3227]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * EVENT: member 98 Oñate: 18 AUG 1598 [3228] [3229]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * EVENT: small of stature, gray bearded, having a wart on the left side of the face.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Appearance: 1598 [3230] 
2 CONT
2 CONT [3224] [S2194] ONMF, p. 112
2 CONT
2 CONT [3225] [S2194] ONMF, p. 112
2 CONT
2 CONT [3226] [S2200] ORIGINS OF NM FAMILIES
2 CONT
2 CONT     * PAGE: pg. 86
2 CONT
2 CONT [3227] [S2200] ORIGINS OF NM FAMILIES
2 CONT
2 CONT     * PAGE: pg. 86
2 CONT
2 CONT [3228] [S2200] ORIGINS OF NM FAMILIES
2 CONT
2 CONT     * PAGE: pg. 87
2 CONT
2 CONT [3229] [S2162] New Mexico's First Colonists
2 CONT
2 CONT     * PAGE: pg. 22
2 CONT
2 CONT [3230] [S2200] ORIGINS OF NM FAMILIES
2 CONT
2 CONT     * PAGE: pg. 86
2 CONT
2 CONT [113663] [S2200] ORIGINS OF NM FAMILIES
2 CONT
2 CONT     * PAGE: pg. 87
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Juan and his family were a members of Don de Oñate party that came to settle New Mexico in 1598.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was listed as 40 years old, the son of Simón Pérez and a native of Mexico City. He was described as small in stature, gray-bearded and having a wart on the left side of his face. With him came his wife, two sons and seven daugh
2 CONC ters. One son, Simón, was listed as a soldier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan's wife was María de la Cruz. Both Juan and his wife were living in 1626. Besides Simón, they had another son named Diego. Dieg adopted the surname of Santa Crus. Their known daughters were Ana, fifty in 1631 and married to As
2 CONC encio de Arechuleta; Yuma, who was forty in 1631, and married to Antonio Baca; Beatriz, who was thirty-eight in 1631 and married to Alonso Varela. ~The Origins of New México Families, pg. 87
2 CONT
2 CONT Oñate lead a party into Kansas to search for the legendary Gran Quivara. Upon his return, he found that 400 colonists had deserted San Gabriel, the Spanish colony begun by the Oñate party. Those who remained were considered loyali
2 CONC sts, and stayed on to raise their families in New Mexico. Juan was among the Loyalists who stayed in New Mexico. ~New México's First Colonie
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT He was a soldier accompanying Don Juan de Oñate on the Oñate Expedition of 1598-1608, and a founding resident of Santa Fe, Nueva España.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT    Was Member of 1598 Ornate Expedition into New Mexico. Appearence in 1598 small of stature, gray bearded wart on left side of face.
2 CONT
2 CONT --
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his wife are said to have been a possible source of a hereditary disorder called El Frio, or Common Hispanic Mutation, a form of cerebral cavernomia (CCM)
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.npr.org/2013/08/20/213728245/researchers-use-genetic-history-to-answer-mutation-questions?t=1592236049649
