1 NAME Antonio /Baca/
2 GIVN Antonio
2 SURN Baca
2 _MARNM Baca
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1589
2 ADDR
3 CITY Ciudad de México
3 STAE Reino de México
3 CTRY Reino de Nueva España
1 DEAT
2 DATE 21 JUL 1643
2 ADDR
3 CITY Santa Fe
3 STAE Nuevo México
3 CTRY Nueva España
1 OCCU Beheaded leader of Santo Domingo Rebellion., Soldier
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Antonio Baca was born circa 1589. He was married to Yumar Pérez de Bustillo who was born c.1591. They had three daughters: Gertudris, who married Antonio Jorge; Ana, who married Francisco López de Aragón, and Anton
2 CONC ia, who was the wife of Antonio de Albizu. It does not appear that Antonio had any sons to carry on the Baca name. Antonio was the main ring-leader in the anti-Rosas faction which caused the Governors death. He was also the leader
2 CONC  of the people who defied the Governor by barricading themselves with the Friars at Santo Domingo Pueblo. His turbulent career ended when he was be-headed along with seven others July 21, 1643. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT He came with his family when they moved to New Mexico from New Spain in 1600. His family were among the Loyalists who stayed in New Mexico.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Antonio Ortíz Baca, was a Capitán by 1628 and was twenty-six years old, he said, married and living in Santa Fé. He was the main ring-leader in the anti-Rosas faction which caused this Governor's death, January 25, 1642. He was a
2 CONC lso the leader of the people who defied the Governor by barricading themselves with the friars at Santa Domingo pueblo. His turbulent career ended on July 21 1643 when he was beheaded with others in Santa Fé.
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife was Yumar Pérez de Bustillo, forty years old in 1631. She, too, had come to New Mexico as a child with her parents. Of their three known daughers, Gertrudis married Antonio Jorge, Ana was the wife of Franciso López de Ara
2 CONC gon, and Gregoria married Antonio de Alvizu. What later Bacas were their sons, if any, cannot as yet be ascertained."
2 CONT
2 CONT Angélico Chávez
2 CONT
2 CONT ONMF p.10
2 CONT
2 CONT Antonio married Yumar Pérez de Bustillo, daughter of Juan Pérez de Bustillo and María de la Cruz. (Yumar Pérez de Bustillo was born in 1591 in Ciudád de México, Nueva España 252 and died after 1643.)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/110326636/person/120122573222 Ancestry Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Aug 23 2017, 12:00:06 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/110326636/person/120122573222 Ancestry Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 12 2017, 12:49:58 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT According to the book  ''Chavez-A Distinctive American Clan of New Mexico'',  Antonio Baca joined several irate colonial leaders and others, who went and murdered Governor Luis de Rosas on January 25, 1642 for exploiting padres se
2 CONC rving the Mission Indians as well as any colonists who openly opposed him. Antonio was subsequently condemned to death as a traitor and was beheaded in Santa Fe on July 21, 1643. All others were pardoned the next day.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Antonio the son of Cristobal Baca I and Ana Ortiz, and brother to Alonso Baca was a captain by 1628 was married and living in Santa Fe.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to A. Chaves he was the main-ringleader of the anti-Rosas faction. He was more into politics than his brother Alonso.He was elected to the Santa Fe Cabildo or city council about 1640 and it was at this time he and others
2 CONC  became incensed over the corruption of Gov. Luis Rosas and the governor's anti-cleric stance.
2 CONT
2 CONT General Alonso Pacheco de Heredia (1642-44) arrived from Mexico to replace Rosas but the anti-clerical  faction( Antonio Baca was the leader of the anti Rosas clique who defied  Rosas by barricading themselves with the friars at S
2 CONC anto Domingo Pueblo). This clique was compelled to take immediate and violent action ahead of Pacheco's  arrival when Nicolas Ortiz wife was found to be living with Rosas and pregnant by him. Nicolas, to say the least ,was  outrag
2 CONC ed. And so he and seven  other masked captains broke into Rosas home and murdered him. The assassination of Luis Rosas(1638-1641) took place on January 25, 1642.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicolas Ortiz, a native of Zacatecas, Nueva Espana anda 16 year old young soldier who came to New Mexico in 1634. He  married  Maria Perez de Bustillo the niece of  Antonio Baca and the daughter of his brother Alonso Baca and  Yum
2 CONC ar Perez de Bustillo. Antonio became the friars champion in their struggle with the state authorities. But while out doing his job escorting wagon trains to and from Mexico City and Santa Fe his wife was living with in Rosas's hom
2 CONC e and obviously with child. Maria testified that she had been having an affair with Rosas for four years. It was he who burst into Rosas' house where was He was being held and stabbed him several times with his sword. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Antonio, related through marriage to Nicolas Ortiz and  as head of the Cabildo did his best to shelter  and protect Nicolas. But the powers that be in Mexico City perceived the entire event as a prelude to sedition and demanded sw
2 CONC ift justice, and this they got. Gov. Pacheco was ordered to have Nicolas put on trial and if found guilty, executed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antonio Baca was chosen to preside over the trial of Nicolas and un surprisingly, was found not guilty. But on his way to Mexico City with the records of the proceedings Nicolas  was taken into custody enroute to be retried by the
2 CONC  governor of Nueva Vizcaya in Parral. This time he was found guilty and was to hanged and his severed head and sword hand were  displayed to be displayed on the gibbett. But lady luck was once again on Nicholas's side. He somehow 
2 CONC escaped and was never seen of or heard of again. But his fellow conspirators were not so fortunate, not by a long shot.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antonio Baca, along with his brother-in-law Juan de Archuleta I and several other relatives and associates in the anti-Rosas clique found themselves in the summer of 1643 in jail  by the order of the new governor General Alonso Pa
2 CONC checo y Herida and sentenced to death.
2 CONT On July 21, 1643, Antonio, the ring-leader, and seven others  were beheaded at the  Plaza at Santa Fe. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Custos or head friar Hernando de Covarrubias administered the last rites to the eight men facing death. It was gory. When Francisco de Salazar's executioner tried to behead him with own daggar but they made a horrible job of i
2 CONC t.  In agony Francisco cried out " sharpen that thing and put me out of my misery!" The custos claimed , absurdly so, that his severed head recited the"entire true and essential creed of the Roman Catholic faith!" Antonio's head w
2 CONC as nailed to the gibbett. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The property of the conspirators' property was seized and the proceeds paid for a peacekeeping force of 30 men enlisted that very day. The governor sought to put to death 14 more for treason but all managed to keep their necks inc
2 CONC luding Alonso Baca but he did spend some time in prision.Alonso was Antonio's younger brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT                                       BACKGROUND
2 CONT Gov. Rosas was anti-clerical, a typical stance taken by the kingdom of  Nueva Mexico governors in the 17th century. When two friars confronted him he attacked them physically  calling  them liars, pigs, traitors, and heretics. The
2 CONC  church was razed in 1640 and the adobes and other materials used in other construction projects.Rosas was excommunicated for his actions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rosas murder took place shortly after the untimely death of his successor, Juan Flores de Sierra y Valdez. Flores had named his Sargento Mayor Francisco Gomez as Lt. Gov. shortly before his death  but the newly elected cabildo of 
2 CONC Santa Fe led by Antonio Baca and packed with associates ( military-clerical alliance) who felt that Gov. Rosas had denied them a more powerful role in governing  the province. They refused to recognize  Gomez's authority and in de
2 CONC fiance of colonial law declared the cabildo the ruling authority. Rosas was imprisoned and the plot to do away with him took shape. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When Gov. Pacheco arrived in November of 1642 he carried with the authority to issue a public pardon to all those who had taken part in the events of that year.  But he also carried the authority to get rid of the conspirators  by
2 CONC  a brief and exemplary punishment.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Pueblo Revolt of 1680 by Andrew L.Knaut p.208 fn32
