1 OCCU Soldier (around 1640 to 1646)
1 TITL Capitán
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Captain '''Francisco Lopez de Aragon''' born c. 1635 at Nuevo Mexico, Nueva España, died before 1661 at Nuevo Mexico, Nueva España
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: 
2 CONT #[http://www.geni.com/people/Ana-Baca/6000000001500817169?through=6000000000400787567 Ana Ortiz Baca] daughter of Antonio Ortiz Baca and Yumar Pérez de Bustillo
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Francisco and Ana Ortiz Baca:
2 CONT #[http://www.geni.com/people/Juana-Lopez-de-aragon/6000000008019749036?through=6000000000400787567 Juana Lopez de Aragon]
2 CONT #[http://www.geni.com/people/Ana-Aragon/6000000001500820153?through=6000000000400787567 Ana Lopez de Aragon]
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1642 Francisco acted as the attorney for Nicolas Ortiz, assassin of former New Mexico Governor Luis Rosas.  He is mentioned among the soldiers escorting the wagon-train from Mexico City in 1640 and 1646.  By 1661, he is referre
2 CONC d as dead.
2 CONT
2 CONT His widow, Ana Baca, a sister of Antonio Jorge's wife, lived in her "estancia del Álamo" about four leagues from Santa Fé. A daughter, Juana, married to Sebastián de Herrera Corrales, was massacred at Taos, togther with his mother
2 CONC . Another daughter, Ana, was the wife of Francisco Campusana, both were still living in 1682. There were no males of this familiy mentioned in the Revolt lists or later. This was the end of the López de Aragón line, which was dist
2 CONC inct from the one which came from México City in 1692.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~Origins of New México Families, pgs. 54-55
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/110326636/person/120122572091 Ancestry Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 12 2017, 12:49:33 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/110326636/person/120122572091/facts Ancestry Genealogy] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 12 2017, 12:50:26 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Francisco Lopez de Aragon or simply de Aragon is mentioned among the soldiers escorting the wagon-train from  Mexico City in 1640 and 1646.  In 1642 he acted as attorney for Nicolas Ortiz. (3)  Ana Baca, a sister of Antonio Jorge'
2 CONC s wife, lived in her 'estancia del Alamo' about four leagues from Santa Fe.  Governor Mendizabal berated for being devoted to the Franciscans, also claiming that she owned him money borrowed for her daughter's wedding.(2)  A daugh
2 CONC ter Juana, who was married to Sebastian de Herrera Corrales, was massacred at Taos with her mother and it seems a brother (3)  Another daughter, Ana, was the wife of a Francisco Campusano, both still living in 1692.(4)  No male Ar
2 CONC agons are mentioned in the revolt lists or later.  This was the end of this family, entirely distinct from the one which came from Mexico City in 1693.
