1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Information provided by Joseph H. Sanchez  9th grandson
2 CONT
2 CONT Killed in Taos, New Mexico by Indians during the Pueblo Revolt of 1680.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fray Francisco Munos (Sanchez de Inigo)was assigned to Nueva Mexico from 1660 to 1680. It has been determined that he had three children by Juana Lopez de Aragon. The three children had previously been reported by Fray Angelico an
2 CONC d others to have been the children of Ana [Juana de Aragon] Lopez  her sister. Juana de Aragon was married to Sebastian de Herrera Corrales
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/110326636/person/120091169119 Ancestry Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 12 2017, 12:49:01 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/110326636/person/120091169119/facts Ancestry Genealogy] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 12 2017, 12:49:33
2 CONT  UTC'
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Information from Joseph H. Sanchez 9th great grandson
2 CONT
2 CONT Information from " The St. Lawrence Day Massacre Victims" (The Pueblo Revolt) by Luis Brandiner
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1680 her mother Ana Lopez de Aragon decided to visit Taos, with her daughter Juana Lopez de Aragon, her unnamed son and Juana's husband Sebastian de Herrera Corrales. On August 10th Sebastian was away from the house when the In
2 CONC dian attack began in Taos, killing Ana, her daughter Juana and her unnamed son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Major Sebastian Herrera Corrales and Don Francisco Chaves escaped from Taos and reported the deaths of all the Spaniards in the jurisdiction - "something more than seventy persons"'
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT A History of Discovery  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Sánchez de Iñigo Puzzle
2 CONT New Genealogical Considerations
2 CONT
2 CONT By Josá Antonio Esquibel
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The genealogy of the Sánchez de Iñigo family of seventeenth-century New Mexico has been a puzzle for researchers interested in uncovering the paternal origins of this distinct New Mexican family. Fray Angélico Chávez identified th
2 CONC e progenitor of the Sánchez de Iñigo family as Juana López, the mother of Jacinto Sánchez de Iñigo and Pedro Sánchez de Iñigo, and presumably the mother of doña Francisca Sánchez de Iñigo.  A pre-nuptial investigation record for P
2 CONC edro referred to his mother, Juana López, and did not offer any clues as to the identity of his father. Similar records for Jacinto Sánchez de Iñigo and doña Francisca Sánchez de Iñigo do not identify the names any parents for the
2 CONC se individuals. The origin of the Sánchez de Iñigo surname used by these individuals has stumped New Mexico genealogical researchers for the past fifty years, particularly because there have not been any records extracted for a se
2 CONC venteenth-century settler of A New Mexico with this surname. As such, It has been thought that the Sánchez de Iñigo children were perhaps the children of one or more men living in New Mexico during the latter half of the 1600s. No
2 CONC w, recent research into seventeenth-century Inquisition records pertaining to New Mexico has lead to uncovering records for a man who was accused of "living scandalously" with a woman named Juana López de Aragón in 1663, and the s
2 CONC urname this man's mother was Sánchez de Iñigo.
