1 OCCU Wagon Train Master, Occupation	 Chirrionero de lo carros 1603 (Age 9 years)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Francisco de Madrid came to New Mexico in 1603, and brought 10 new soldiers and four friars along with him. In 1626 he boasted that he was one of the "ancient settlers." At this time he is mentioned as the brother-
2 CONC in-law of Pedro Márquez. His wife was María de la Vega Márquez.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1639, he was a Captain when he gave his age as forty-six. In 1641, Francisco was a member of the Santa Fé Cabildo, or town council.
2 CONT
2 CONT His children to all appearances, were: Francisco II, Francisca, wife of Juan Varela de Losada; and María, nicknamed "Mariaca." An Alférez, Cristóbal de Madrid, who escorted some colonists to New Mexico in 1641, might have been ano
2 CONC ther son, or perhaps the elder Francisco was meant since Cristóbal is not heard of again.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~The Origins of NM Families, pgs. 65-66
