1 NOTE {geni:about_me} IN THE PHILIPPINES HE IS KNOWN IN HISTORICAL ACCOUNTS AS MANUEL DE LEON, 27TH GOVERNOR GENERAL OF THE PHILIPPINES WHERE HE DIED. THIS ERROR WAS DONE BY CLAIMANTS OF THE DUCHY OF ARCOS IN 1780S:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.geni.com/photo/view/6000000009242240340?album_type=photos_of_me&end=&photo_id=6000000181157359831&project_id=&start=&tagged_profiles=
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 24, 1669, Manuel de Leon took possession of the Philippines as new governor-general. He was appointed by royal provision on June 24, 1668 and arrived in Manila on September 24, 1669.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his time the seeds of cacao were brought to the Philippines and planted first in Carigara, Leyte. De Leon extended the commerce of the islands to China, India, and Java, and thus enabled the citizens of Manila to attain unu
2 CONC sual wealth and prosperity.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 11, 1677 (according to Concepcion's account from his Hist. de Philipinas, vii, pp. 258, 259), Governor Manuel de Leon died due to excessive obesity. De Leon left all his property for charitable purposes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Casimiro Diaz from his Conquistas said that Governor General Manuel de Leon died on the night of April 8, 1676. Below is a direct extract of Diaz account in Pages 157-161 of The Philippine Islands, 1493-1898 Volume XLII, (1670-170
2 CONC 0), edited by Blair, on how de Leon died:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://kahimyang.com/kauswagan/articles/1277/today-in-philippine-history-september-24-1669-manuel-de-leon-took-possession-of-the-philippines-as-new-governor-general
2 CONT
2 CONT At this time, in the year 1675, Governor Don Manuel de León was in great danger of dying, on account of having placed himself under medical treatment, without being actually sick, solely for the sake of improving his health—a proo
2 CONC f that it might have cost him his life. Don Manuel was a corpulent man, and had grown so fleshy that he was almost unable to move about without aid, at which he grieved much because he could not attend to many functions which belo
2 CONC nged to the obligations of his office. In view of this hindrance and his desires, Juan Ventura Sarra (whom I have already mentioned in the voyages to Siam and the coast of Coromandel) bound himself to cure Don Manuel and remove fr
2 CONC om him that great encumbrance [of flesh]—confident because he was a very expert surgeon, and the governor a man of great courage and reared in and accustomed to the perils of war. The governor accordingly accepted this treatment; 
2 CONC and the skillful surgeon opened his abdomen in many places and removed from him many lumps of fat, and then sewed up and treated the wounds. In a few weeks the governor became well, and his flesh was much reduced, to the wonder of
2 CONC  those who saw how the surgeon cut the flesh from his body, and the courage which the governor displayed—and what caused most dread [of the result] was his being an aged man, but little less than seventy years old. The king of Leó
2 CONC n, Don Sancho I, was cured about the year 920 of a similar infirmity of excessive obesity, by the physicians of the Moorish king of Córdoba, Abderramen; but their treatment was not so harsh and sanguinary. It is certain that Juan 
2 CONC Ventura Sarra was a great surgeon, and showed that he was such not only with this governor, but also in the year 1682 with his successor, Master-of-camp Don Juan Vargas Hurtado. There was no hope for Don Juan’s life, on account of
2 CONC  a large abscess in the hip, which was not understood to be such by the physicians; but Juan Ventura knew what it was, and opened the abscess with a large lancet which he made from a dagger, more than a tercia long, since the cavi
2 CONC ty was very deep. In this operation he showed his skill as much as Don Juan de Vargas displayed his great bravery and endurance, which aroused admiration.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the cure of Don Manuel de León was so marvelous, he did not, since that inordinate obesity was now a disease and a corruption of nature, long enjoy the agility and lightness of body that the medical treatment had obtained
2 CONC  for him; and so he gradually fell back into that unusual infirmity, and again found himself, as before, without the use of his limbs. He had many wounds in his body, which he had received in more than fifty years of military life
2 CONC  in Flandes, Alemania, and Galicia, where he had taken part in battles more celebrated than were known in those times [i.e., of which Diaz was writing]. He had been so courageous in not fearing dangers that they called Don Manuel 
2 CONC de León "Ironhead." Among these he had one deep wound, which must have been imperfectly or only apparently healed; and this in course of time, and with the pressure on it that would be caused by the increase of flesh, opened, a gr
2 CONC eat flow of blood issuing from it. This occurred so inopportunely that he was present in the church of Santo Domingo, clothed in mourning garb, assisting in the funeral rites for Doña María Cuéllar, wife of Auditor Don Francisco d
2 CONC e Coloma. His blood flowed very copiously, but those near him could not see it on account of the mourning garments, and because the chair and cushion were of black, until he began to swoon, and sank into the chair. They carried hi
2 CONC m in their arms to his coach, and thence he was conveyed to his palace, where all the care due to the cure of such a personage as he was furnished. The above-mentioned Juan Ventura Sarra treated him, applying all means which the a
2 CONC rt of surgery imparts to those who are so skillful as was Juan Ventura, who within four months brought him to what seemed a state of convalescence. But as his age was so great, and could not give much aid to the medicine (which on
2 CONC ly assists nature), Don Manuel could never regain sound health. The physicians ordered him to go to one of the houses that stand by the river opposite Manila, where he spent a long time—until, on the night of April 8, 1676, they f
2 CONC ound him dead in his bed, although he had retired without any indications of such danger. They found a power of attorney authorizing the father provincial of St. Dominic, Master Fray Diego de San Román, to make a will in his name,
2 CONC  and directions that he be interred in the royal chapel of the Incarnation belonging to the soldiers of Manila, where he lies in a little chapel which stands on the gospel side. He was one of the best governors who has ruled these
2 CONC  Filipinas Islands, very disinterested, pious, affable, and clement; and his death was therefore regretted by all classes. The estate that he left was the only property belonging to a governor that was put to good use,28 the relig
2 CONC ious who acted as administrator applying it to pious works which the governor had named to him—such as the holy Bureau of La Misericordia, so that for years many orphan girls were given in marriage by means of that part [of the go
2 CONC vernor’s donation] which belonged to their dowries, until, with the successive wrecks of the two galleons "Santo Cristo de Burgos" and "San José," in the years 1693 and 1695, the principal of that great endowment was entirely cons
2 CONC umed. He also left directions to found a well-endowed chaplaincy in his native place—Paredes de Nava, in the district of Campos—and many other good works, worthy of his piety.
2 CONT
2 CONT On account of his death, the senior auditor, Don Francisco de Coloma, took charge of the government, in company with auditors Don Francisco de Mansilla and Don Diego Calderón y Serrano for civil affairs.
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT Pambansang Komisyong Pangkasaysayan
2 CONT The Philippine Islands, 1493-1898, Volume XLII, 1670-1700, page 15, 157-161 E. H. Blair, Gutenberg EBook #34384
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT HIS DESCENDANTS IN THE PALAWAN, PHILIPPINES UNTIL NOW:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT THAT EVEN MANUEL PONCE DE LEON, THE 27TH GOVERNOR GENERAL OF THE PHILIPPINES, THE GRANDFATHER OF PABLO PONCE DE LEON WAS ERASED IN HISTORY BY ENEMIES OF THE PONCE DE LEON IN SPAIN BY OMITTING THE WORD PONCE. THUS CREATING A DIFFER
2 CONC ENT PERSON. JESUITS HISTORICAL ACCOUNTS CLEARLY IDENTIFIED HIM AS MANUEL PONCE DE LEON, THE GRANDFATHER OF PABLO. PABLO PONCE DE LEON WAS ALSO ERASED IN HISTORY BY THE INTEREST GROUPS WHO CLAIMED THE DUKEDOM OF ARCOS. BY NOT RECOG
2 CONC NIZING THE FIRST FAMILY OF DON ANTONIO'S SONS , PABLO AND RODRIGO. ONLY RECORDED IS THE LAST WIFE OF ANTONIO WHOM HE MARRIED WHEN HE AS 52 YEARS OLD. ANTONIO MARRIED FIRST AT HIS TEENAGE... WITH 2 SONS. IN THE SECOND WIFE PRODUCED
2 CONC  NO CHILDREN AT ALL DUE TO OLD AGE.
2 CONT
2 CONT PLEASE BE CLARIFIED THAT PABLO AND RODRIGO ARE VICTIMS OF INTEREST GROUPS WHO ASSUMED THE DUCHY WHILE THEY WERE EXILES.
2 CONT
2 CONT CLAIMING THE DUCHY BACK IS NOT THE ISSUE HERE BUT JUST THE CORRECT LINEAGE.
2 CONT
2 CONT THE ANTONIO PONCE DE LEON II WAS BORN IN CUYO, PHILIPPINES WHERE THE TOMB OF PABLO WAS STILL PRESENT AT THE VERY ALTAR OF THE CUYO SACRED CHURCH.
2 CONT
