2 ADDR
1 OCCU Calvary Captain/Syndic of the Franciscans, Captain of the Cavalry, Regent Procurator General, Captain
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Captain '''Andres Hurtado''' born January 1, 1620 at Puebla de Zaragoza, Nueva España, died c. 1649 at Heroica Puebla de Zaragoza, Nueva España
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Settlers of 17th Century New Mexico 1601-1680.
2 CONT
2 CONT El Camino Real de Tierra Adentro brought wagon trains of good to trade as well as new settlers into New Mexico. The Spanish government kept strict control over travelers coming into and leaving New Mexico. Very few family groups a
2 CONC rrived in New Mexico in this period. Mainly single men were the new settlers. They married the daughter and granddaughters of the earlier settlers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrés is first mentioned in 1661 as a captain thirty-three years old. He was born in the city of Zacatecas and was then living in the Sandía District. He was a captain of the cavalry and Syndic of the Franciscans in 1664. He was 
2 CONC married to Bernardina de Salas y Trujillo, also known as Bernardina de Salas y Orozco.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the encomiendas of Santa Ana and neighboring Pueblos. He was persecuted by Governor López Mendizábal for his friendship with the Friars. He was dead before 1693, but his widow and several of his children escaped the Pueblo
2 CONC  Revolt of 1680 and later returned with Diego de Vargas in 1693.
2 CONT
2 CONT • Census: Don Diego de Vargas, 1692, El Paso, The Pueblo de Nuestra Senora de Guadalupe del Paso, Nuevo Méijico, Nueva España. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fourteenth household
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrés Hurtado, married to Antonia Domínguez. He states that he ready, as his majesty's loyal vassal, to enter immediately to settle New México, when I, the governor and captain general enter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal Crown Restored, pg. 41
2 CONT
2 CONT • Census, 1 May 1697, Santa Fé, Santa Fé, Nuevo Méjico, Nueva España. 317 Dona Bernardina de Salas, widow, Her daughters, Bernarda, Bernardina; 4.5 varas of lana, 3.75 of bayeta, 9 mantas
2 CONT
2 CONT • Dates & Events. 250
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrés Hurtado held a grievance against Governor don Bernado López de Mendizabal for having been recalled as an official of the Moqui province and compelled under penalty of death to appear before the governor in Santa Fe within t
2 CONC en days of the summons, being forced to travel in the extreme cold of winter during January 1661 with his wife (Bernardina de Salas y Trujillo) and five children, ages nine and younger. In this year he held the position of a regid
2 CONC or (town councilman) of the Villa Santa Fe, and his house where he resided with his wife and children was sixteen leagues from Santa Fe in the area of the Pueblo of Zia. The governor contended that as an encomendero Hurtado was re
2 CONC quired to maintain a house in the Villa de Santa Fe. In addition, Hurtado in the Pueblo of Santa Ana and this was prohibited by law.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hurtado received the governor's order on January 29, 1661 while in the Moqui Province, a distance of 100 leagues from the Villa de Santa Fe. He left the Moqui area in the company of the friar who was the visitador of the province 
2 CONC to travel to his home. From his home he then traveled to the Pueblo of Zia with his wife and five children, the oldest being nine years of age. Despite the extreme winter weather, which he referred to as "tan rigoroso y de tantas 
2 CONC niebas" ("very harsh and with much snow"). They traveled sixteen leagues (approximately 39 miles) on "el camino" and along the way his youngest daughter, just over four months of age, suffered frostbite of one of her feet, and the
2 CONC  trip caused his wife ill health.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrés Hurtado further mentioned that he had come to New Mexico twelve years earlier, "en dose años qe estoy en esta reyno" (circa 1649), and that he always remained "querido y estimado de todos los becinos y religiosos desta reyn
2 CONC o como es publica vos y fama" ("loved and esteemed by all the vecinos and religious of this kingdom, as is well-known publically"). He stated he did not have a house in Santa Fe because he was unable to sustain and support himself
2 CONC  and his family in the villa. He lost livestock due to severe winter weather and had to move himself and his family into the house of Sargento Mayor Francisco de Madrid for two months. He mentioned he lost 84 head of livestock and
2 CONC  was robbed of 80 fanegas of wheat.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrés Hurtado signed his declaration, which was dated October 17, 1661, Santa Fe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Researcher: The Honorable Don José Antonio Esquibel
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: AGN, Tierras, t. 3268, f. 60vff.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrés married Bernardina de Salas y Trujillo, daughter of Diego de Trujillo and Catalina Marquez Vásquez, By 1652 in Bernalillo, Nuevo Méjico, Nueva España. (Bernardina de Salas y Trujillo was born about 1635 in Santa Fé, Santa F
2 CONC é, Nuevo Méjico, Nueva España and died on 2 Feb 1729 in Santa Fé, Santa Fé, Nuevo Méjico, Nueva España.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: After Bartolome de Salazar died, Diego de Trujillo took the three sisters, María de Salazar Hurtado, María Josefa de Hinojos and Isabel de Salazar Hurtado from their mother, María de Hinojos, to be raised and adopted by his 
2 CONC granddaughter Bernardina Salas y Trujillo and her husband Andres Hurtado.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1661 Captain of the Sandia District, 1664 Captain of Cavalry, Regent Procurator General and Syndic for the Franciscans. In 1662 adopted Bartolome de Salazar's 3 Daughters Maria de Salazar Hurtado, Isabel Hurtado de Salazar and Mar
2 CONC ia Josefa de Hinojos said to be his daughters by unknown Indian woman. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/110326636/person/120091188225 Ancestry Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 29 2017, 12:49:52 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Source: LDS microfilm #0283371 (Marriage Investigations, Sagrario, Zacatecas) —this microfilm contains an early book of marriages dating from 14 February 1606 - 7 November 1619).
2 CONT
2 CONT __________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT HURTADO - SALAS y TRUJILLO
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrés Hurtado (ONMF: 49) held a grievance against Governor don Bernardo López de Mendizábal for having been recalled as an official of the Moqui province and compelled under penalty of death to appear before the governor in Santa
2 CONC  Fe within ten days of the summons, being forced to travel in the extreme cold of winter during January 1661 with his wife [Bernardina de Salas y Trujillo] and five children, ages nine and younger. In this year he held the positio
2 CONC n of a regidor (town councilman) of the Villa de Santa Fe, and his house where he resided with his wife and children was sixteen leagues from Santa Fe in the area of the Pueblo of Zia. The governor contended that as an encomendero
2 CONC , Hurtado was required to maintain a house in the Villa de Santa Fe. In addition, Hurtado lived at the Pueblo of Santa Ana and this was prohibited by law. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hurtado received the governor’s order on 29 January 1661 while in the Moqui Province, a distance of 100 leagues from the Villa de Santa Fe. He left the Moqui area in the company of the friar who was the visitador of the province t
2 CONC o travel to his home. From his home he then traveled to the Pueblo of Zia with his wife and five children, the oldest being nine years of age. Despite the extreme winter weather, which he referred to as “tan rigoroso y de tantas n
2 CONC iebas“ (“very harsh and with much snow”), they traveled sixteen leagues (approximately 39 miles) on “el camino” and along the way his youngest daughter, just over four months of age, suffered frostbite of one of her feet, and the 
2 CONC trip caused his wife ill health.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrés Hurtado further mentioned that he had come to New Mexico twelve years earlier, “en dose años qe estoy en esta reyno” (circa 1649), and that he always remained “querido y estimado de todos los becinos y religiosos desta reyn
2 CONC o como es publica vos y fama” (“loved and esteemed by all the vecinos and religious of this kingdom, as is well known publicly”). He stated he did not have a house in Santa Fe because he was unable to sustain and support himself a
2 CONC nd his family in the villa. He lost livestock due to severe winter weather and had to move himself and his family into the house of Sargento Mayor Francisco de Madrid for two months. He mentioned he lost 84 head of livestock and w
2 CONC as robbed of 80 fanegas of wheat. Andrés Hurtado signed his declaration, which was dated 17 October 1661, Santa Fe.
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife has been identified by fray Angélico Chávez as Bernardina de Salas y Trujillo (also known as Bernardina de Salas y Orozco). Chávez indicated that Andrés Hurtado was referred to as a son-in-law of Diego Trujillo (ONMF: 10)
2 CONC . The original source (AGN Tierras, t. 3268, f. 94) refers to Capitán Diego de Trujillo as an encomendero and then mentions “su hijo Capn Franco de Trujo y sus yernos Capn Xptoval Vaca y Capn Andres Hurtado.” This record clearly i
2 CONC ndicates that the wives of Cristóbal Baca and Andrés Hurtado (Ana Moreno de Lara and Bernardina de Salas y Trujillo, respectively) were daughters of Capitán Diego de Trujillo, and thus they were sisters of Francisco de Trujillo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fray Angélico Chávez referred to Bernardina de Salas y Trujillo as a daughter of Francisco de Trujillo, but did not provide a specific source citation for this statement. It appears that there may have been some confusion that res
2 CONC ulted in the inconsistency with his previous statement about Andrés Hurtado being his son-in-law. Chávez also mentioned that Bernardina de Salas was a “granddaughter of María de Vera,” but once again did not give a specific source
2 CONC  citation for the statement.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Based on the declaration of Andrés Hurtado that he arrived in New Mexico in 1649 and was married and had a family of five children in 1661, the eldest of which was nine years of age (born circa 1652), we can estimate the year of 
2 CONC marriage with Bernardina de Salas y Trujillo as circa 1649-1651. As such Bernardina de Salas y Trujillo was a contemporary of Francisco de Trujillo, rather than his daughter. This further supports the statement that Andrés Hurtado
2 CONC  was a son-in-law of Diego de Trujillo.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: LDS microfilm #0283371 (Marriage Investigations, Sagrario, Zacatecas) —this microfilm contains an early book of marriages dating from 14 February 1606 - 7 November 1619).
2 CONT
