2 TITL Andrés  Hurtado (1628-1680) | WikiTree FREE Family Tree
1 NOTE Andrés Hurtado (ONMF: 49) was a native of Zacatecas, born circa 1628. There is a record of veiling found in the Zacatecas marriage book for Pedro Hurtado and Catalina de Salazar dated 17 July 1617. The record reads: "En diez y sei
2 CONC s de Jullio del año de seiscientos y diez y siete aso velo El Licdo Dio de herra y Atriaga [Diego de Herrera y Arteaga] cura bndo. a po hurtado y a catalina de salazar, fueron sus padrinos Juo co--cais [illegible] y Juana maria su
2 CONC  mujer vso [vecino] de esta ciudad".
2 CONT
2 CONT If it can be proven that Andrés Hurtado, native of Zacatecas and a settler of New Mexico in the seventeenth century, was a son of Pedro Hurtado and Catalina de Salazar, this would offer an explanation for the use of the Salazar su
2 CONC rname by his daughters (Lucía de Salazar, Isabel de Salazar, María de Salazar —ONMF: 49).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Further research into land records and civil records of Zacatecas may produce some promising results regarding the Hurtado family.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Researcher: José Antonio Esquibel
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: LDS microfilm #0283371 (Marriage Investigations, Sagrario, Zacatecas) —this microfilm contains an early book of marriage dating from 14 February 1606 - 7 November 1619).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1664 he was a captain of cavalry and also Syndic
2 CONT of the Franciscans. Andres also held the encomiendas of Santa Ana and neighvoring purelbos; in this
2 CONT capacity he was cruelly persecuted by Governor Lopez Mendizabal for his friendship with the friars. Though a regent adn a procurator-general of the Kingdom, he was made to take his entire family from
2 CONT Sandia to Santa Fe i the dead of winter, in 1661. He was dead when fateful 1680 came around, but his
2 CONT widow and several of her children not only escaped the massacre but returned with the Reconquest in
2 CONT 1693.
