1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Simón de Abendaño''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Simón was born in Ciudád de Rodrígo on the border of Portugal and Spain and emigrated to the New World and then to New México during the 1600s or 1610s. The name Abendaño has a history of association with Jewish families in Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among Sephardic Jews with the name Abendaño, there are records of Mayr Abendanno as selling sheep and goats to the city's slaugter house in Murcia in 1469. Also in 1469. Mose Abendanno was apprehended along with many other armed J
2 CONC ews attempting to enter the Moorish quarter of the city in search of one of Mose's servants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Herónimo Abendaño and his wife Beatríz Riquelme are listed on the 1660 census of Lorco Spain. They lived next door to Juan de Osca Alzamora and Juliana Real de Aguilar who were parents of a New Méxican ancestor, Alonso Rael de Agu
2 CONC ilar.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~To the End of the Earth, pg.137-138
2 CONT
2 CONT Stanley M. Hordes, Ph.D, To the End of the Earth, A History of the Crypto-Jews of New México,
2 CONT
2 CONT Published by Columbia University Press, New York, 2006.
2 CONT
2 CONT --
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Simon was previously reported to have been the son, apparently illegitimate, of Juan Lopez Holguin and a woman known only by her last name, Abendaño. Simon was a 1/2 brother of Isabel and Ana Holguin and to Cristobal Lopez H
2 CONC olguin. (See Beyond New Mexico...)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Simon de Abendano was either son or son-in-law of Juan Lopez Holguin and Catalina de Villanueva
