1 OCCU Conquistador, Occupation  Alferez Real and also High Sheriff 1631 (Age 34 years) Villa de Santa Fe, New Mexico, Spain, Religious Affiliation Jewish Converso
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Bartolomé Romero, II''' christened April 5, 1557 at the church of Nuestra Senora de la Asuncion, Corral de Almaguer, Castille La Mancha, España deid 1632 Nuevo México
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: 
2 CONT #Lucia Lopez Robledo born about 1573 in España died about 1625.  Lucia was the daughter of Pedro Robledo and Catalina Lopez.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Bartolomé and Lucia Lopez Robledo
2 CONT #Bartolome lll
2 CONT #Matias
2 CONT #Agustin
2 CONT #Ana, who married Francisco Gomez
2 CONT #Maria, who married Gaspar Perez.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bartolomé and his wife were members of the Oñate expedition to New Mexico in 1598.  Bartolomé was Oñate’s Captain of Artillery.  At this time he is described as "having a good stature, dark and black-bearded."  He was quickly prom
2 CONC oted to captain after their arrival in New Mexico.  He was 35 in 1598.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Strong evidence from archives of the inquisition indicate that the Romero's who came to New Mexico were Jewish coversos,who continued to live under church suspicions of Jewish practices.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his book "To the End of the Earth," Stanley Hordes speculates that Bartolome Romero, one of Juan de Onate's officers, was a New Christian and possibly a crypto-Jew. Romero was from the small La Mancha farming community of Corra
2 CONC l de Almaguer. This village apparently had a large Jewish community including a number of Romeros who in church records were described as New Christians. In addition, Romero's mother Maria de Adeva may have been related to a promi
2 CONC ntent Jewish family known as the (Ben)adevas. Hordes also believes that Romero's father in law Pedro Robledo may have also been a crypto-Jew based on his family history.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Bartolomé Romero was another colonist who may be traced to probable Jewish origins in Spain. Born in the eastern La Mancha town of Corral de Almaguer in 1557,35 Romero was one of the first officers to enlist with Onate and can be 
2 CONC found in the muster rolls of both Ulloa and Salazar, accompanied by his wife, Lucía Robledo, daughter of Pedro Robledo and Catalina López. He is described as the son of Bartolomé Romero and as being "of good stature, swarthy [and]
2 CONC  black bearded." He brought with him an impressive array of supplies for the expedition, including a cart with oxen, a plowshare, three coats of mail, two swords and a javelin, a lance, a harquebus, a pistol, twenty horses, some w
2 CONC ith armor, and six mules.36 Romero served as one of Oñate's most trusted captains, representing the governor in his relations with the Pueblo Indians and testifying on his behalf before hostile royal officials. When some disgruntl
2 CONC ed soldiers conspired to desert the colony, Romero actively opposed them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Documents pertaining to Romero's hometown strongly suggest Jewish roots for the family. Before the expulsion of the Jews in 1492, the small town of Corral de Almaguer (population 1,200 in 1575)38 included a Jewish population worth
2 CONC y of note.
