2 CONT
2 CONT He appeared on the Diego de Vargas El Paso Area Muster census from Dec 1692 to Jan 1693 in Pueblo la Ysleta del Sur, Nuevo Méjico. Nueva España. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nineth household, two sons, his mother-in-law, one of her daughters
2 CONT
2 CONT Agustín de Salazar, married to Felipa de Gamboa, with two sons named Antonio, four and Miguel, two. Likewise, living in this house were his mother-in-law, named Lucía Martín, with one daughter, a young woman named Petrona Domíngue
2 CONC z, sixteen. He states that being given a way to settle that kingdom, he will go with his family
2 CONT
2 CONT # RESIDENCE: Santa Cruz de la Cañada 1723
2 CONT
2 CONT # RESIDENCE: Santa Cruz de la Cañada 1721 
2 CONT
2 CONT # BIRTH: 1665, Zuni Pueblo, New Mexico 
2 CONT
2 CONT # DEATH: 17 FEB 1740, Santa Cruz de la Cañada, New Mexico 
2 CONT
2 CONT # EVENT: member '93 Vargas: 4 OCT 1693 
2 CONT
2 CONT # EVENT: as an interpreter, "proficient in his mother's tongue"
2 CONT
2 CONT Even though he was blind, he used his languge ability to warn of the impending Indian resistance that developed when Vargas was waiting for the Tanos to vacate Santa Fe. He had overheard the Indians talking about how they would de
2 CONC feat Vargas and informed Miguel Lujan who relayed the information to Vargas at 4 A.M. The fact that he was in Santa Fe in Dec of 1693 proves that he and his family made the trip north with the first reconquest settler party. The p
2 CONC hrase, 'his mother's tongue," seems to negate Fray Angelico's suggestion that he might have been Apache and supports the fact that his mother, Maria de Hinojos, was indeed pueblo (Zuni) .
2 CONT
