1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://www.santafenewmexican.com/LocalNews/Women-of-history Women make mark in Santa Fe history]
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1606-1607, Hernán fathered a son by a Tano Indian woman named '''Doña Inés'''. Because of a lack of records, it is not certain if Hernán remarried after the death of his first wife or if his relations with Doña Inés were out
2 CONC side of marriage. In either case, Doña Inés apparently resided at the camp of Santa Fe when she gave birth to Hernán Martín Serrano, el mozo (the younger), who gave his birthplace as Santa Fe in later years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It appears that Doña Inés was the same woman who was taken as a young girl from the Tano Pueblo of San Cristóbal in 1591 when the Spaniards of the Castaño de Sosa expedition left New Mexico. Raised among Spaniards, she became accu
2 CONC lturated and accustomed to the ways of Spanish society. Returning to New Mexico as a member of Oñate's expedition, Doña Inés was expected to serve in a role similar to that of La Malinche, the Indian interpreter who aided Hernán C
2 CONC ortés 80 years earlier. Doña Inés became so well-assimilated that she lived among the Spaniards in the camp of Santa Fe, becoming the mother of one of the first recorded individuals born of mixed Spanish and Pueblo Indian parentag
2 CONC e. 
2 CONT
