1 OCCU group of women who defended their homes January 1599 (Age 17 years) San Juan Pueblo, New Mexico, Spain Note: Only 4 months after the colony had arrived in Nueva Mexico and expedition was sent out to punish the inhabitants of Acoma
2 CONC  for the death of Maese d
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From the chapter on Don Fernando Duran y Chaves I and Dona Maria Carvajal Holguin in "Chávez: A Distinctive American Clan of New Mexico" by Angelico Chavez
2 CONT
2 CONT https://books.google.pl/books?id=YcpIZIOBu4gC&pg=PA22&lpg=PA22&dq=Juan+de+Vitoria+Carvajal&source=bl&ots=Aqh3w8fpNR&sig=2BBUdihDP-ALpX9F0RK9llJLPNc&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiR2PvZ7rDWAhXHI1AKHXL5BEcQ6AEIYjAO#v=onepage&q=Juan%20de%20V
2 CONC itoria%20Carvajal&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. His Helpmate and Estancia
2 CONT
2 CONT Now to Fernando Duran y Chaves' wife, [we have] precious little to go  on. In fact, we do not even have her given name. She must have always stayed down at El Tunque, away from  the social and political turmoil in Santa Fe, that s
2 CONC he finds no mention in the records. Only once was she referred to indirectly when Agustin de Carvajal, the man associated with her husband Don  Fernando in the Rosas murder affair, was identified as being her brother-in-law. Hence
2 CONC  she was a Carvajal, and whom we now christen "Maria," this name implied by long Spanish custom as the first part of almost every girl's baptismal name. As Agustin's sister, she was then the daughter of one of Don Juan de Onate's 
2 CONC favorite men, Juan de Vitoria Carvajal, who had come to New Mexico in his entourage of 1598. And so we have a great deal about her family background as for Don Fernando's own mother Isabel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan de Vitoria Carvajal was described in the 1598 lists as an alferez 37 years old, the son of Juan de Carvajal and born at Ayotepel in the Marquisate of the Valley (of Mexico). He was of medium stature and wore a chestnut beard.
2 CONC  His surname had been a most prominent one down there since the Cortes Conquest, and one can see him hinting at some superior ancestry by specifying his birthplace's association with Cortes' title of marques. Among the latter's me
2 CONC n there had been an Antonio Carvajal from Zamora in Spain, and the ancestry of a Luis de Carvajal was linked with that of his present leader, Don Juan de Onate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (There was also a younger officer with exactly the same long name, but having a different father, Alonso Ruiz de Guzman. The two could have been first cousins, both bearing their grandparental name of Vitoria. It is not known if t
2 CONC his man had any family, or if he stayed in New Mexico. In 1609, he  escorted Governor Peralta to Santa Fe, and he finds last mention in 1617 as an alferez 30 years old in association with the older Vitoria Carvajal, a capitan at t
2 CONC he time and 55 years of age.)
2 CONT
2 CONT As for  this older Carvajal, he had always enjoyed Onate's trust, who had sent him back to New Spain to bring the second colonial batch of colonists in 1600, the one in which Don Pedro Duran y Chaves came, as well as his wife's Va
2 CONC ca family. Here Carvajal was again described as formerly, with the added notation that he was well-featured, and having a mark above his left eye. While he was the alcalde mayor of Santa Fe in 1614, he was accused by others of hav
2 CONC ing illegally assumed authority when he ordered an Indian assassin executed while Governor Peralta was in prison (during the Ordonez affair of the previous year). Then in 1622 he was mentioned as the sindico of the Franciscans, th
2 CONC eir term for the layman who handled their material affairs. Here he referred to himself as a married man and a "first founder of the land." In 1629, as the Alferez Real for the Holy Office, he accompanied Padre Perea, a really fin
2 CONC e Custodio of the friars, in a memorable procession which went out of Santa Fe to welcome an incoming new lord governor, Don Francisco Nieto de Silva.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime after 1600, Carvajal married an Isabel Holguin, who herself could boast of no mean background. Her father, Juan Lopez Holguin, was also an alferez when he came in 1600 with his wife and family. The latter's name was Cater
2 CONC ina de Villanueva, who, like some housewives of her day, dabbled in home-remedies for lack of physicians and the like. (In 1626 local gossip accused her of trafficking in "magic roots".) Lopez Holguin himself had been described as
2 CONC  being of good stature and black-bearded ith a scar over one eye, and 40 years old, the son of Juan Lopez Villasana, and a native of Fuente Ovejuna in Extremadura. (This town, which I purposely visited, lies not far east from Valv
2 CONC erde de Llerena, hence Lopez Holguin and Don Pedro Duran y Chaves must have often exchanged reminiscences of their native Etramadura.) Nothing else is known about him save that in 1626, when he gave his age as 46, he reminded othe
2 CONC rs that he was a "founder of the kingdom."
2 CONT
2 CONT His eldest son, Simon, went by the name of Abendano, evidently his child by a previous wife in Spain, since he gave Ciudad Rodrigo as his birthplace; he had then married Maria Ortiz, a daughter of Cristobal Vaca, and both were dea
2 CONC d by 1622. Their only known child, Maria de Abendano, married a Diego de Vera in this same year, with Governor Sotelo and Dona Isabel de Bohorques standing as witnesses. A younger son was Cristobal Holguin. And then there was Isab
2 CONC el Holguin, who became the wife of Juan de Vitoria Carvajal.
2 CONT
2 CONT --
2 CONT
2 CONT From In the Dust of Time: A Novel Based on a True Series of Events, by Donald L. Lucero
2 CONT
2 CONT https://books.google.pl/books?id=31l9AwAAQBAJ&pg=PT286&lpg=PT286&dq=Olguin+Carvajal&source=bl&ots=nttI2sZHjd&sig=8a6n1BAUSzek2z00WY9Da50mcSA&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwi5i9Kw6rDWAhXNaFAKHQlRAeY4ChDoAQg3MAM#v=onepage&q=Olguin%20Carvajal
2 CONC &f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT Research notes given in references within Chapter 28: An Aborted Attempt
2 CONT *5. Isabel Holguin. Almost certainly of a converso family, Isabel was the daughter of Juan Lopez Holguini and Catalina de Villanueva, wife of another converso, Juan de Vitoria Carvajal. A description of the endogamous marriage pat
2 CONC terns exhibited by New Mexico's colonists of probably New Christian ancestry (Aberdano, Carvajal, Gomez, Holguin, Jorge, Ortiz, Robeldo, Romero, Vera, etc.) is provided by Hordes. See "To The End of the Earth" pp. 137-45.
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogy:
2 CONT *Juan de Vitoria Carvajal and Isabel Holguin.
2 CONT **Through their daughter Juana de Carvajal - Juana de Carvajal and Juan Lucero de Godoy (see "August 13th" 2, Juan Lucero de Godoy.)
2 CONT *** Through their daughter Melchora de Carvajal - Melchora de Carvajal and Cristobal Olguin
2 CONT ****Salvador Holguin and Magdalena Fresqui
2 CONT *****Ana Lopez Olguin and Juan de Vargas Machuca
2 CONT ******Rosa de Vargas Machuca and Blas Martin Serrano (see "Notes for Appendix 1" 5, Domingo Martin Serrano).
2 CONT
2 CONT Catalina de Villanueva being the daughter of a Indian Chief Don Joseph, taught her son Juan de Vitoria Carvajal her native tongue. When Luis de Carvajal came to colonize Nueva Leon, Juan de Vitoria became his soldier and interpret
2 CONC er of native languages . 
