0 @N6000000005024494848@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Oñate Conquistador of 1598. His brother was killed in the attack by the Acomans in December, 1598.
1 CONT Son of Juan Ruiz, native of Cartaya, of good stature, chestnut colored beard, 36 years of age.
1 CONT ___________________________
1 CONT Hernando de Hinojos (Ynojos) and his brother Sebastián appear in the 1597 Casco roll of Oñate's forces as natives of Cartaya, Condado de Niebla, the sons of Juan Ruiz. Hernando was thirty-six years old in 1598, when he was describ
1 CONC ed as having a good stature and a chestnut beard; here again he was mentioned as a native of Cartaya, the son of Ruiz, and brother of Sebastián Rodrituez. He might be the Hernando "Yñiquez" who escorted Father Jiménez to New Mexic
1 CONC o in 1608. As a captain, then also Procurator General for the Kingdom, he accompanied the wagon-trains in 1613 and 1617. His brother Sebastián was killed at Acoma in 1598. 
1 CONT Hernando's wife was Beatriz Pérez de Bustillos, who was mentioined as his widow by 1632. A known son of thiers was Miguel and a daughter, Gerónima, was the wife of Francisco de Anaya, also, it seems, another Hinojos by the name of
1 CONC  Juan.,
0 @N6000000005024494824@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Miguel de Hinojos, son of Fernando (Hernando), held the Alcaldía of Jemez until Governor Mendilzábal took it from him prior to 1663. He acted as bondsman for Nicolás Ortíz in 1642. In 1661 he claimed the encomienda of Humanos Pueb
1 CONC lo by reason of his being the son of "one of the Conquistadores, Hernando de Hinojos." He also said that he had a brother [Juan?], whose name he did not mention. Miguel also owned lands on the Santa Fe River about a league south o
1 CONC f the Villa.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is not known if he was married or, if so, who his wife was. A daughter of his, María, wife of a certain Juan de Vega, seems to have had an Apache mother.
1 CONT Bondsman for assassin Nicolas Ortiz in 1642. Alcalde of Jemez until 1662.
0 @N6000000005024488751@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Antonio Ortíz Baca, was a Capitán by 1628 and was twenty-six years old, he said, married and living in Santa Fé. He was the main ring-leader in the anti-Rosas faction which caused this Governor's death, January 25, 1642. He was al
1 CONC so the leader of the people who defied the Governor by barricading themselves with the friars at Santa Domingo pueblo. His turbulent career ended on July 21 1643 when he was beheaded with others in Santa Fé. 
1 CONT
1 CONT His wife was Yumar Pérez de Bustillo, forty years old in 1631. She, too, had come to New Mexico as a child with her parents. Of their three known daughers, Gertrudis married Antonio Jorge, Ana was the wife of Franciso López de Ara
1 CONC gon, and Gregoria married Antonio de Alvizu. What later Bacas were their sons, if any, cannot as yet be ascertained.
0 @N6000000005024488562@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT AKA: Gertrudis Baca Pérez
0 @N6000000005024488729@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Pedro Varela passed muster in 1598 with his brother Alonso. Pedro was six years younger, twent-four years of age, and gave the same father and birthplace as Alonso. He had a good stature and a red beard. Nothing more is known abou
1 CONC t him, save that his son Pedro Varela II, active in the major campaign in 1638, was referred to as a cousin of Alonso Varela.
1 CONT Pedro Varela passed muster in 1598 with his brother Alonso. Pedro was six years younger, twent-four years of age, and gave the same father and birthplace as Alonso. He had a good stature and a red beard. Nothing more is known abou
1 CONC t him, save that his son Pedro Varela II, active in the major campaign in 1638, was referred to as a cousin of Alonso Varela.
