1 CONT Diego de Trujillo first appears in New Mexico as an Alférez and farmer, nineteen or twenty years old, in 1632. He was in the soldier-escort of 1641. In 1662 he was fifty, a Sargento Mayor, living in the jurisdiction of Sandía as L
1 CONC ieutenant General for the Rio Abajo area, as well as Alcalde Mayor of Zuñi. He then declared that he was born in México City. His wife was Catalina Vásquez.
1 CONT ______________________________
1 CONT In 1662, Captain Diego de Trujillo (ONMF: 107) was identified as an encomendero in a suit that he brought against Governor Don Berbardo López de Mendizábal, Trujillo, demanding to be paid 868 pesos by the governor for livestock an
1 CONC d Apache servants. In his claim there was mention of his son, Captain Francisco de Trujillo, and two son-in-laws: Captain Cristóbal Baca (husband of Ana Moreno de Lara, aka Moreno de Trujillo) and Antonio de Carbajal. This informa
1 CONC tion indicates that Truijillo had a second daughter whose name is not known at this time, but who was the wife of Antonio de Carbajal. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The particular passage from Diego de Trujillo's claim that contains the information cited above reads: "..a su mujer y a su hixo el Capn Franco de truxillo y a dos yernos suyos que son el Capn Xptobal Vaca y anto de carbajal digo 
1 CONC el Capn Andrés Hurtado y demando le cantd de ochocientos y sesenta y ocho pesos de reses y ugues mulas y cavallos y mil ocho piezas de Yndios Apaches."
1 CONT
1 CONT Researcher: José Antonio Esquibel
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: Archivo General de la Nación (AGN), México, Galeria, Concursos de Peñalosa, Vol. I, f. 235/382. Microfilm copy of rolls #1-3 (Vols. I-III) of the "Concursos de Peñalosa" located at the New Mexico Records Center and Archive
1 CONC s.
1 CONT
1 CONT ______________________________________
1 CONT Diego de Trujillo (ONMF: 107-08), the progenitor of the Trujillo family of New Mexico, identified himself as a native of México City. Fray Angélico Chávez noted that in 1632, Trujillo gave his age as 19 or 20, and in 1662 he gave 
1 CONC his age as 50, indicating he was born circa 1611-12. In addition, Chávez informs us that Trujillo and his wife, Catalina Vásquez had a son named Francisco de Trujillo and a daughter named Ana Moreno de Lara, wife of Cristóbal Baca
1 CONC  (ONMF: 10 & 108). For the purpose of the promising lead presented below, it is also worthwhile to note that a daughter of Francisco de Trujillo was named Bernardina de Salas y Oroszco, aka Bernardina de Salas y Trujillo (ONMF: 49
1 CONC  & 108).
1 CONT
1 CONT In the early 1600s, there was a couple named Francisco de Trujillo Villvicencio and Doña Maríana de Salas Jorosco (Orozco?) who had been residents of the Pueblo of Chalco (located 25 miles southeast of México City) in Nueva España
1 CONC  before coming to reside in the town of Tacubaya near México City. This couple had the following two know sons: Alonso Moreno de Lara (aka Moreno de Trujillo) and Nicolás de Trujillo.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alonso Moreno de Lara was married in the town of Tacubaya, then a part of the parish jurisdiction of Santa Vera Cruz Church in México City. He was married on 4 October 1638 with Doña Estefania de Ayala y Manrique. In this marriage
1 CONC  record, Alonso's parents were named as "Franco de truxillo Villvicencio" and "Da Maríana de Salas Jorosco." Alonso and Doña Estefania had several children who used these combination of surnames: Moreno, Manrique de Trujillo, Manr
1 CONC ique de Lara, and Moreno de Trujillo.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nicolás de Trujillo, also a native of the Pueblo of Chalco and a resident of Tacubaya, was married 24 February 1646, Santa Vera Cruz Church, México City, with Inéz de Trejo.
1 CONT
1 CONT Could it be that Diego de Trujillo, the progenitor of the Trujillo family in New Mexico, was another son of Francisco de Trujillo Villvicencio and Doña Maríana de Salas Jorosco? The year of marriage of Alonso Moreno de Lara indica
1 CONC tes that he and Diego de Trujillo were at least contemporaries, if not brothers. Also, as is apparent, the use of a variety of similar surnames in both families (de Trujillo, Moreno de Lara, Salas y Jorosco/Orozco) certainly offer
1 CONC s a lead that is well worth following up on in this case. 
1 CONT
1 CONT For the interested researcher, a good place to begin is in attempting to locate and review records of the Trujillo Villavicencio-Salas y Jorosco family from the Pueblo of Chalco, as well as combing through records from Santa Vera 
1 CONC Cruz Church for information pertaining to other possible members of this family.
1 CONT
1 CONT For additional material on the Trujillo family see BONMF Vol. 2
1 CONT
1 CONT Researcher: José Antonio Esquibel
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: México, Distrito Federal, Ciudiad de México, Santa Vera Cruz Church, Matrimonios, 1568-1666, LDS # 0035848., ,
0 @N6000000005024494268@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Francisco de Trujillo was the only son of Diego de Trujillo and Catalina Vásquez. It is not known which daughter of María de Vera, or Baca (step-daughter of Antonio de Salas) he married, but it could have been a Doña Lucía de Mont
1 CONC oya mentioned in 1663. (Her mother was the second wife of Diego de Montoya.) This Lucía seems to be the "Doña Luisa de Trujillo" whose hacienda, the present site of Alburquerque, is mentioned years later.
1 CONT
1 CONT They had a daughter named Bernardina de Salas y Trujillo, wife of Andrés Hurtado, whose older daughters married several prominent and prolific colonists before and after the Reconquest. Several male Trujillos mentioned at the time
1 CONC  of the Rebellion, and after, must have been his children or grandchildren, there being no other known Trujillo at this period.
1 CONT Francisco de Trujillo was the only son of Diego de Trujillo and Catalina Vásquez. It is not known which daughter of María de Vera, or Baca (step-daughter of Antonio de Salas) he married, but it could have been a Doña Lucía de Mont
1 CONC oya mentioned in 1663. (Her mother was the second wife of Diego de Montoya.) This Lucía seems to be the "Doña Luisa de Trujillo" whose hacienda, the present site of Alburquerque, is mentioned years later.
1 CONT
1 CONT They had a daughter named Bernardina de Salas y Trujillo, wife of Andrés Hurtado, whose older daughters married several prominent and prolific colonists before and after the Reconquest. Several male Trujillos mentioned at the time
1 CONC  of the Rebellion, and after, must have been his children or grandchildren, there being no other known Trujillo at this period.
1 CONT __________________________________
1 CONT Although there was no formal villa in the area, by the 1650s several dozen Spanish estancias (farms), were scattered throughout the valley. Among them was the home of Francisco de Trujillo and his wife, Doña Luisa, located on the 
1 CONC east bank of the Rio Grande amid a large stand of cottonwoods, near present-day Old Town. The property, which came to be known as El Bosque de Doña Luisa (the Grove of Doña Luisa), was chosen by New Mexico's governor in 1706 to be
1 CONC  the site of the Villa de Alburquerque.
0 @N6000000005024481562@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Bernardina de Salas, simply, or de Salas y Trujillo (or Orozco), was a grand-daughter of María de Vera, one of whose daughters had maried Francisco de Trujillo. The Hurtado children were numerous: Lucía de Salazar, third wife of J
1 CONC uan Lucero de Godoy; María de Salazar, later married to Manuel Baca; and their Hurtado brethren, Andrés, Diego, Francisco, Martín, Maríana, who later married Manuel Vallejo; Catalina, later the wife of José Gallegos; and Juana, ca
1 CONC ptured by the Indians in 1680 and rescued by her brother Martín in 1692.
1 CONT Known as Bernardina de Salas or de Salas y Orozco.
