0 @N6000000005024494424@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Alonso Baca,  a contemporary of Antonio, was to all appearances Antonio's younger brother. As a young Captain, he lead a small exploritory expedition three hundred leagues into the eastern plains in 1634. He gave his age as 55 in 
1 CONC 1644, but must have been a year or two younger. In the middle of the century, he uncovered a serious Indian plot. He was one of fourteen conspirators ordered executed by Governor Pacheco in 1643 (when Antonio died), but all these 
1 CONC fourteen escaped death; Alonso was still living at his place in Rio Abajo district as late as 1662. Nothing more is known about him, not even his wife's name. Cristóbal Baca, mentioned in 1663 as sobrino carnarl of Antonio Baca, w
1 CONC as apparently his son. 
1 CONT ______________________________________
1 CONT When the pueblos rebelled in 1680, there were only four male adult male Bacas listed: Cristóbal, Igacio, Manuel, and José.
0 @N6000000005024494055@ NOTE Catholic
0 @N6000000005024494023@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Matías López del Castillo was thirty-five years old in 1626.
1 CONT He was married to the daughter of Ana de Bustillos and living in Santa Fé. His wife was therefore an Archuleta, daughter of Asencio. Matías was also in the soldier-escort of 1628. One daughter of his seems to be have been Ana Lópe
1 CONC z del Castillo, the wife of Juan de Herrera. After the Reconquest there were more family matches between this Herrera family and that of Pedro López del Castillo.
1 CONT ____________________
1 CONT A fragment of the will of Matías López del Castillo (ONMF: 55) was recently uncovered by Rob Martínez in the collection of the Achivo Histórico del Parral. The fragment contains information about the place of birth López del Casti
1 CONC llo and the names of his parents. Chávez identified López del Castillo as having been born circa 1591. In his will, dated 1632, Matías declared he was a native of Cabra, in the province of Córdoba and located about 35 miles southe
1 CONC ast of the City of Córdoba. Cabra lays between the Sierra de la Cabra and the Sierra de Montilla in the region of Andalucía.
1 CONT
1 CONT Matías López del Castillo named his parents as Juan del Castillo and María Días (Díaz) de Cuéllar. Information about the wife and children of Matías were not located in the fragment, but the available information makes clear refer
1 CONC ence to the fact that he was in New Mexico prior to making his will. If he died soon after making the will, he was about 41 years of age when at the time of his death. Cháves indicated that one of his daughters was apparently Ana 
1 CONC López del Castillo, the wife of Juan de Herrera.
1 CONT
1 CONT Researcher: Robert Martínez
1 CONT
1 CONT Narrative Summary: José Antonio Esquibel
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: Archivo Histórico del Parral, Reel 1632, Frame 180.
0 @N6000000005024481687@ NOTE Catholic
0 @N6000000005024481657@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Matías Romero, second son of old Bartolomé, and most likely born before his parents reached New Mexico, was Alférez Real and also High Sheriff of Santa Fé in 1631, when he refused to testify against his brother-in-law, Gaspar Pére
1 CONC z. His wife was Isabel de Pedraza, cousin of María de Archuleta, wife of Juan Mparquez. In 1644, he and Juan Gómez de Luna were accused of trading illicitly with the Plains Indians and making captives for Governor Rosas. Matías di
1 CONC ed in Santa Fé about the year 1648. The descendants of this couple can be identified later on by their use of "de Pedraza" with their Romero name.
1 CONT Bartolomé and Francisco Romero de Pedraza of the next generation were in all probability their sons.
1 CONT
1 CONT The testimony of Diego Pérez Romero [before the tribunal of the Inquisition in Mexico City] offers valuable genealogical information on the first three generations of the Romero family in New Mexico. Of particular note are the cle
1 CONC ar genealogical links that can now be made between members of the Romero family that previously could not be confirmed, although logic dictated possible connections. This is particularly true for the Romero de Pedraza family and t
1 CONC he Romero de Salazar family. Fray Angélico Chávez supposed that Bartolomé Romero de Pedraza and Francisco Romero de Pedraza were sons of Matías Romero and Doña Isabel de Pedraza. As it turns out, the other children were Pedro Rome
1 CONC ro, Felipe Romero, Catalina Romero, and Luisa Romero, the wife of Juan Lucero de Godoy. This information now allows descendents of Felipe, Catalina, and Luisa to make the genealogical connection to the founders of the Romero famil
1 CONC y in New Mexico that has eluded researchers for many years.
1 CONT
1 CONT Researcher: José Antonio Esquibel
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: José Antonio Esquibel, "The Romero Family of Seventeenth Century New Mexico," Part 1, forthcoming in Herencia, January 2003,
0 @N6000000005024481696@ NOTE Francisco Romero de Pedraza (ONMF: 388) was referred to as "an uncle of Lucia Gomez Robledo, daughter of Andres Gomez Robledo." Actually, Andrés Gómez Robledo would have been first cousin of Francisco Romero de Pedraza if we follo
1 CONC w Chávez's lead that Francisco was in all probability the son of Matías Romero (ONMF: 97) and Isabel de Pedraza. Matías Romero was a son of Bartolomé Romero and Luisa Robledo and the brother of Ana Robledo who was married with Fra
1 CONC ncisco Gómez, the parents of Andrés Gómez Robledo (ONMF: 36-37, 95-97).
1 CONT
1 CONT Note: This is an example of the Spanish custom to refer to the children of ones first cousin as niece or nephew.
1 CONT
1 CONT Researcher: Virginia Sánchez
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: Fray Angélico Chávez, Origins of New Mexico Families in the Spanish Colonial Period, Museum of New Mexico Press, Santa Fé, 1992.
0 @N6000000005024481625@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Felipé Romero, Capitán, escaped in 1680 with his wife and six sons, one of military age and the rest small, as well as four grown daughters. In 1681 he said that he was forty-two years old and married, and was pictured as slender,
1 CONC  of good stature, with long straight hair. This time he presented his eldest son, Sebastián.
1 CONT In 1661 Felipé had been accused, along with Bartolomé Gómez Robledo, of killing some cattle that belonged to Alamillo Pueblo. He and his wife, Jacinta de Guadalajara y Quirós, lived near this Pueblo at their hacienda of San Antoni
1 CONC o de Sevilleta. She was twenty-seven in 1667.
1 CONT Their eldest son, Sebastián, passed muster in 1681 with his father. He was seventeen, tall and thin, with a long face and somewhat thick lips. Two known daughters were Juana, who married Antonio Dominguez de Mendoza, and isabel, w
1 CONC ife of Diego del Rio. The last marriage shows that the parents were already dead. The other five sons, small in 1680, and the other two grown daughters, cannot be traced so far. They could be any of the numerous Romeros who return
1 CONC ed with the Reconquest, especially those with "apostolic" names who re-settled the Rio Abajo country.
