0 @N6000000005024488358@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT The Norman adventurer Jean de Bethencourt "The Great" first conquered the Canary islands of  in 1402. He is the proginator of the Bethencourt name which has since undergone several variations including Betancur.
1 CONT
1 CONT Conquest (of the Canary Islands) in earnest only began with the Norman adventurer Jean de Bethencourt who, in 1402 , claimed Lanzarote on behalf of his feudal lord. Henry III of Castile. 
1 CONT
1 CONT All Betancur (and other spelling variations of Bethencourt) families of the Canaary Islands are descended from the Norman French Bethencourt family of Jean de Bethencourt.
0 @N6000000005024502386@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT List of persons who came to New Mexico to reinforce Oñate's colonists:
1 CONT Angeles, Beatriz de los-servant of Cristóbal de Brito.
1 CONT __________________________________________
1 CONT She was a well-known "curandera," or healer (this word has more of a meaning of a sorceress). Note the following documentation of the Spanish Inquisition set up in Mexico City during the early part of the 17th century:
1 CONT
1 CONT The following excerpt regarding the inter-cultural exchanges between Indians and Spaniards in colonial New Mexico is from: The Pueblo Revolt of 1680, by Andrew Knaut, p. 146:
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT One illustrative example of this cultural transmission from Pueblo to Hispanic society is that of Beatriz de los Angeles. A mestiza and the widow of alférez Juan de la Cruz, de los Angeles lived on an estancia amid the Tano pueblo
1 CONC s southeast of Santa Fe. Throughout the 1620's, she enjoyed a reputation among the Hispanic community as a echisera de compación, "sorceress of compassion," or more clearly, a curandera who proffered home remedies and incantations
1 CONC  from the vantage point of one who moved in both Indian and European circles.1  Supporters reconted the tale of how de los Angeles traveled to Senecú in 1628 to help doña María Granillo overcome a serious illness. There, while in 
1 CONC the company of "other witches," the curandera apparently saved her patient's life by playing patoles with Granillo and simultaneously reciting a number of spells.2
1 CONT
1 CONT But de los Angeles's reputed talents involved more than the ability to cure illness through shamanism. For years, women in the Hispanic community had sought her help in curbing the amorous pursuits of wayward husbands. Usually, de
1 CONC  los Angeles answered these requests by prescribing gusanos ciegos, a type of worm gathered easily in any dung heap in the region, and directing her clients to hide them in the food eaten by the men in question.3   Problems arose 
1 CONC for de los Angeles, however, at the close of the decade when she gave this concoction to her own partner, Diego Bellido. Bellido grew violently ill upon ingesting a bowl of milk laced with the worms, and when de los Angeles attemp
1 CONC ted to nullify the potion by giving Bellido an unspecified oil as an emetic, his condition only worsened, leading ultimately to his death. 4  The incident drew the attention of the commissary Estevan de Perea and generated a lengt
1 CONC hy investigation by the Inquisition into de los Angeles's activities.
1 CONT ___________________________
1 CONT Footnotes:
1 CONT 1. Testimony of Petronilla de Zamora, 25 March 1631, Inquisición Tom., 372, AGN(Scholes Transcript, Box 4615).
1 CONT
1 CONT 2. Testimony of Catalina Bernal, 25 March 1631, Inquisición Tom., 372, AGN(Scholes Transcript, Box 4615).
1 CONT
1 CONT 3. See, for instance, the testimonies of Ana Cadimo ( 25 Mar 1631), Juana Sanchez (22 Jun 1631), and Catalina Bernal ( 26 Mar 1631), Inquisición Tom., 372, AGN(Scholes Transcript, Box 4615). Sanchez testified that de los Angeles h
1 CONC ad given her the prescription as early as 1620, while others admitted to following the mestiza's advice in the latter part of the decade.
1 CONT
1 CONT 4. Testimony of Catalina de Bustillos, 26 March 1631, Inquisición Tom., 372, AGN(Scholes Transcript, Box 4615).
0 @N6000000005024488989@ NOTE The following is from "Beyond Origins of NM Families", by José Antonio Esquibel. It would appear that the progenitor of the Trujillo name in NM is most likely related to this Francisco Trujillo:
1 CONT
1 CONT Diego de Trujillo (ONMF: 107-08), the progenitor of the Trujillo family of New Mexico, identified himself as a native of México City. Fray Angélico Chávez noted that in 1632, Trujillo gave his age as 19 or 20, and in 1662 he gave 
1 CONC his age as 50, indicating he was born circa 1611-12. In addition, Chávez informs us that Trujillo and his wife, Catalina Vásquez had a son named Francisco de Trujillo and a daughter named Ana Moreno de Lara, wife of Cristóbal Baca
1 CONC  (ONMF: 10 & 108). For the purpose of the promising lead presented below, it is also worthwhile to note that a daughter of Francisco de Trujillo was named Bernardina de Salas y Oroszco, aka Bernardina de Salas y Trujillo (ONMF: 49
1 CONC  & 108).
1 CONT
1 CONT In the early 1600s, there was a couple named Francisco de Trujillo Villvicencio and Doña Maríana de Salas Jorosco (Orozco?) who had been residents of the Pueblo of Chalco (located 25 miles southeast of México City) in Nueva España
1 CONC  before coming to reside in the town of Tacubaya near México City. This couple had the following two know sons: Alonso Moreno de Lara (aka Moreno de Trujillo) and Nicolás de Trujillo.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alonso Moreno de Lara was married in the town of Tacubaya, then a part of the parish jurisdiction of Santa Vera Cruz Church in México City. He was married on 4 October 1638 with Doña Estefania de Ayala y Manrique. In this marriage
1 CONC  record, Alonso's parents were named as "Franco de truxillo Villvicencio" and "Da Maríana de Salas Jorosco." Alonso and Doña Estefania had several children who used these combination of surnames: Moreno, Manrique de Trujillo, Manr
1 CONC ique de Lara, and Moreno de Trujillo.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nicolás de Trujillo, also a native of the Pueblo of Chalco and a resident of Tacubaya, was married 24 February 1646, Santa Vera Cruz Church, México City, with Inéz de Trejo.
1 CONT
1 CONT Could it be that Diego de Trujillo, the progenitor of the Trujillo family in New Mexico, was another son of Francisco de Trujillo Villvicencio and Doña Maríana de Salas Jorosco? The year of marriage of Alonso Moreno de Lara indica
1 CONC tes that he and Diego de Trujillo were at least contemporaries, if not brothers. Also, as is apparent, the use of a variety of similar surnames in both families (de Trujillo, Moreno de Lara, Salas y Jorosco/Orozco) certainly offer
1 CONC s a lead that is well worth following up on in this case. 
1 CONT
1 CONT For the interested researcher, a good place to begin is in attempting to locate and review records of the Trujillo Villavicencio-Salas y Jorosco family from the Pueblo of Chalco, as well as combing through records from Santa Vera 
1 CONC Cruz Church for information pertaining to other possible members of this family.
1 CONT
1 CONT For additional material on the Trujillo family see BONMF Vol. 2
1 CONT
1 CONT Researcher: José Antonio Esquibel
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: México, Distrito Federal, Ciudiad de México, Santa Vera Cruz Church, Matrimonios, 1568-1666, LDS # 0035848.
0 @N6000000005024494256@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Lucía de Montoya had a daughter named María de Tejeda. They were both residents of Santa Fé in early 1662. María de Tejeda maintained an affair with Luis Durán, also a resident of Santa Fé, by whom she had two children, according 
1 CONC to the testimony of Juan Estebán de Fagoaga given at Santa Fé in April 1662. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [Note: Juan Esteban de Fagoaga was a native of the Valle de Ollarzu in the Province of Vizcaya (Spain), age 55 in April 1662 (b.ca. 1607). He was a single man who was a vecino of the jurisdiction of Sandia and lived 12 leagues (ap
1 CONC prox. 36 miles) from Santa Fé at the "estancia de Santiago." At the time of his testimony at Santa Fé on 8 April 1662, Fagoaga was very ill. He died May 18, 1662 and was buried on May 19th in the church of Sandia by fray Salvador 
1 CONC de Guerra.]
1 CONT
1 CONT Researcher: José Antonio Esquibel
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: Archivo General de la Nación, Mexico, Inquisición, tomo 593, f. 320.
1 CONT Lucía de Montoya had a daughter named María de Tejeda. They were both residents of Santa Fé in early 1662. María de Tejeda maintained an affair with Luis Durán, also a resident of Santa Fé, by whom she had two children, according 
1 CONC to the testimony of Juan Estebán de Fagoaga given at Santa Fé in April 1662. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [Note: Juan Esteban de Fagoaga was a native of the Valle de Ollarzu in the Province of Vizcaya (Spain), age 55 in April 1662 (b.ca. 1607). He was a single man who was a vecino of the jurisdiction of Sandia and lived 12 leagues (ap
1 CONC prox. 36 miles) from Santa Fé at the "estancia de Santiago." At the time of his testimony at Santa Fé on 8 April 1662, Fagoaga was very ill. He died May 18, 1662 and was buried on May 19th in the church of Sandia by fray Salvador 
1 CONC de Guerra.]
1 CONT
1 CONT Researcher: José Antonio Esquibel
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: Archivo General de la Nación, México, Inquisición, tomo 593, f. 320.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lucía de Montoya, presumably the same woman of this name identified by Fray Angélico Cháves as a possible daughter of Diego de Montoya (ONMF: 77), was the mother of María de Tejeda. In April 1662, Juan Esteban de Fagoaga denounced
1 CONC  María de Tejeda to the Inquistion. Fagoaga declared that she gave birth out of wedlock to two children fathered by Luis Durán. Montoya, Tejeda and Durán were identified as vecinos of Santa Fe.
1 CONT
1 CONT Researcher: José Antonio Esquibel
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources: AGN, Inquisición, t. 593, f. 317.,
0 @N6000000005024502499@ NOTE Catholic
0 @N6000000005024481484@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Andrés Hurtado is first mentioned in 1661 as a Capitán thirty-three years old. He was born in the city of Zacatecas and was then living in the Sandía district. In 1664 he was a Capitán of Cavalry and also Syndíco of the Franciscan
1 CONC s. His wife was Bernardina de Salas y Orozco. Andrés also held the encomiendas of Santa Ana and neighboring pueblos; in this capacity he was cruelly persecuted by Governor López Mendizábal for his friendship with the friars. Thoug
1 CONC h a regent and a procurator-general of the Kingdom, he was made to take his entire family from Sandía to Santa Fé in the dead of winter, in 1661. He was dead when fateful 1680 came around, but his widow and several of her children
1 CONC  not only escaped the massacre but returned with DeVargas in the Reconquest of 1693.
1 CONT
1 CONT ______________________________________
1 CONT
1 CONT Andrés Hurtado (ONMF: 49) was a native of Zacatecas, born circa 1628. There is a record of veiling found in the Zacatecas marriage book for Pedro Hurtado and Catalina de Salazar dated 17 July 1617. The record reads: "En diez y sei
1 CONC s de Jullio del año de seiscientos y diez y siete aso velo El Licdo Dio de herra y Atriaga [Diego de Herrera y Arteaga] cura bndo. a po hurtado y a catalina de salazar, fueron sus padrinos Juo co--cais [illegible] y Juana María su
1 CONC  mujer vso [vecino] de esta ciudad".
1 CONT
1 CONT If it can be proven that Andrés Hurtado, native of Zacatecas and a settler of New Mexico in the seventeenth century, was a son of Pedro Hurtado and Catalina de Salazar, this would offer an explanation for the use of the Salazar su
1 CONC rname by his daughters (Lucía de Salazar, Isabel de Salazar, María de Salazar -ONMF: 49).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Further research into land records and civil records of Zacatecas may produce some promising results regarding the Hurtado family.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Researcher: José Antonio Esquibel
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: LDS microfilm #0283371 (Marriage Investigations, Sagrario, Zacatecas) -this microfilm contains an early book of marriage dating from 14 February 1606 - 7 November 1619).
