0 @N6000000005024494382@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Juan Griego II was already married to Juana de la Cruz in 1626; she was the daughter of Juan de la Cruz, "el Catalán," and Beatriz de los Angeles. In 1661 he declared that he was born in Santa Fé and was a Capitán fifty-six years 
1 CONC old at the time. In this same year he was referred to as being of the same age and having recently returned from a prolonged stay outside New Mexico. Although dead before 1680, he was still remembered after the Reconquest in conne
1 CONC ction with his old homestead at La Cañada.
1 CONT His known children, all Griegos, were Nicolás, Blas, María de la Cruz Alemán, wife of Diego López del Castillo, Graciana, married to Francisco Xavier, and Juana, nicknamed "La Clériga."
0 @N6000000005024481710@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Alferez, native of the town of Corral de Almaguer in Castile, of good stature, swarthy, black beard, 35 years of age. Son of Bartolomé Romero, with complete armor for himself and horse.
1 CONT ______________________________
1 CONT Bartolomé Romero came as an Alférez in 1598. He was thirty-five then, the son of Bartolomé Romero and a native of Corral de Almaguer (east of Toledo, Spain), of good stature, dark, and back-bearded. Already in 1597 he is mentioned
1 CONC  as married to Lucia López (Luisa López Robledo), who came with him and her young family. Bartolomè figured quite prominently in the Oñate annals; he was promoted to Capitán shortly after the colony arrived in New Mexico. His last
1 CONC  recorded act was in 1632 when he reported strange rites performed by the Indians in the church of Alameda Pueblo. 
1 CONT His children were: Bartolomé II, Matías, Agustín, Ana, wife of Francisco Gómez, and María, married to Gaspar Pérez.,
