1 CONT AKA: El Cuate
0 @N6000000005024494083@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Juan González Bernal and his wife, Apolonia, are mentioned in 1663 as belonging to the "Griegos." A daughter of theirs was involved in an affair with a Pedro de Arteaga. As late as 1703, two daughters of Juan González were claimin
1 CONC g Santa Fé property that had once belonged to Isabel Bernal (their grandmother).
0 @N6000000005024494073@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT AKA: Juan González Bernal
1 CONT _____________________
1 CONT In 1696, a group of settlers signed a petition to relocate from La Villa Nueva Santa Cruz de la Cañada to Alameda, and in 1710 Governor Peñuela awarded a large tract of land to Capitán Francisco Montes Vigil. Two years later, Vigi
1 CONC l sold the grant to Juan González who built a chapel and large corrals, some of which were in the area across the river that became known as Corrales. Soon other settlers followed, mostly from Alburquerque.
0 @N6000000005598942998@ NOTE Decendants of Luis II Martin Serrano and Melchora include:
1 CONT
1 CONT Catalina Martin Serrano Salazar- Abt 1677
1 CONT Antonia Martin Serrano- Abt 1678 in Santa Fe, NM/ Abt 1709 and her decendants
1 CONT Maria Rosa Martin Serrano- Abt 1680
1 CONT Catalina Martin Serrano- Abt 1683
1 CONT Sebastian Martin Serrano- Abt 1686
1 CONT Apolonia Martin Serrano- Abt 1689
1 CONT Manuel Martin Serrano Gonzalez- Abt 1693
1 CONT Manuel Diego Martin Serrano- Abt 1697 and his decendants
1 CONT Maria Magdalena Martin- Abt 1700 and her decendants
1 CONT Petrona Martin Serrano- Abt 1702
0 @N6000000003668545394@ NOTE
1 CONT Servant who came to New Mexico with Juan Lopez Holguin in 160 0  "Chavez- A Di s tinctive American Clan of New Mexico, p.23"
0 @N6000000005024502584@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Nicolás de Aguilar was a native of Yurirapundaro, Michoacan, Nuevo España. He was the son of Pedro de Aguilar, deceased, and of Isabel de Villagómez, a native of the same village. His maternal grandparents were Fernando de Villagó
1 CONC mez, an original Conquistador of Michoacan Province, and Luisa Pérez, also born in the same village, When eighteen years old, Nicolás left his widowed mother and went to the northern frontier settlement of Parral, where he worked 
1 CONC as a miner and soldier for six years. From here he fled to New Mexico after committing a murder.
1 CONT Aguilar lived in New Mexico for some time prior to 1660, serving as an unscrupulous lackey of Governor López de Mendizábal. As Alcalde Mayor of the Salinas district he persecuted missionaries with devilish fury and humor. The fria
1 CONC rs referred to him as "Attila." Finally he was summoned before the Inquisition in México City, where he had to answer for his crimes; found guilty, he was banished from New Mexico for ten years and deprived forever of holding any 
1 CONC office.
1 CONT On coming to New Mexico he had married Catalina Márquez, daughter of Francisco Márquez and María Núñez of Santa Fé. At the time of his trial, between 1660 and 1665, they had four children: Gerónima, María, Isabel, and Nicolás. An 
1 CONC Ynez de Gracia, living in the Salinas country near Cuarac, was mentioned as being his sister-in-law. After this, no more is heard of Aguilar. The family either followed him to New Spain, or stayed in New Mexico under their mother'
1 CONC s name, and with good reason. 
1 CONT ______________________
1 CONT 1663: May 11 - In testimony taken from Captain Nicolás de Aguilar on this date, he stated that " ... a pact was made with them (the frontier Indians) that they should not pass beyond the pueblos of Humanos and Tavira (the Salinas 
1 CONC area), when they come to barter; nor should the enemy of the same nation in the jursidiction of Casa Fuerte and Navajo come, because it is from there that the whole kingdom receives hurt, for they (Navajos and Apaches) are all one
1 CONC  people, and it is impossible to tell whether they ae friends or enemies." In late summer of fall of 1663, after being accused of tkaing a crippled girl from Taos, Governor Dionisio de Panalosa Briceno y Berdugo asked why,when it 
1 CONC was known that " ... he had so many Apache (Navajo) captives that he gave away more than a hundred!"34
1 CONT _______________________
